{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.SchurWeyl.Orbit6Braid","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.Orbit6Braid","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalParam"],"declarations":6,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.Orbit6.seminormalParam_mul_rel","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.Orbit6.seminormalParam_mul_rel","kind":"theorem","line":179,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/Orbit6Braid.lean#L179-L193","doc":"**The seminormal parameters satisfy the braid relation's hypothesis.**  With\n`a₁ = a(p)`, `a₂ = a(r)`, `a₃ = a(p+r)` — the three axial distances of a size-`6` orbit,\nwritten multiplicatively as `x = q^{-2p}`, `y = q^{-2r}`, so that the third is at the\n*product* `x*y` — the relation `a₃ (a₁ + a₂ - c) = a₁ a₂` holds identically.\n\nThis is what makes `genA_genB_braid` a statement about the actual Hoefsmit matrices rather\nthan about abstract `6 × 6` arrays. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.Orbit6.genA_quadratic_hecke","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.Orbit6.genA_quadratic_hecke","kind":"theorem","line":167,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/Orbit6Braid.lean#L167-L174","doc":"The Hecke-normalised form of `genA_quadratic`: with `c = q - q⁻¹` and `q ≠ 0` this is\nexactly the relation `Hecke.σ_quadratic` imposes on a generator. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.Orbit6.genA_quadratic","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.Orbit6.genA_quadratic","kind":"theorem","line":152,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/Orbit6Braid.lean#L152-L165","doc":"**The Hecke quadratic relation on the orbit** for `ρ(σ_i)`.  Unlike the braid this is\npair-local — it needs no relation among the parameters, and indeed holds for *every* `c`,\nsince each block has trace `c` and determinant `-1` and Cayley–Hamilton does the rest.\nInstantiating `c = q - q⁻¹` (with `q * q⁻¹ = 1`) gives the Hecke relation\n`σ² = (q - q⁻¹)σ + (q·q⁻¹)·1` in the normalisation of `Hecke.σ_quadratic`.  (`genB`\nsatisfies the same statement with the pairing permuted.) ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.Orbit6.genA_genB_braid","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.Orbit6.genA_genB_braid","kind":"theorem","line":133,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/Orbit6Braid.lean#L133-L150","doc":"**The size-`6` orbit braid identity (Yang–Baxter).**\n\n`ρ(σ_i) ρ(σ_{i+1}) ρ(σ_i) = ρ(σ_{i+1}) ρ(σ_i) ρ(σ_{i+1})` on a generic\n`⟨s_i, s_{i+1}⟩`-orbit, given only the relation `a₃ (a₁ + a₂ - c) = a₁ a₂` that the\nseminormal parameters satisfy (`seminormalParam_mul_rel`).  This is the `6`-dimensional\ncase that does not reduce to the `2 × 2` template, and it is the mathematical core of the\nbraid relation for arbitrary `λ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.Orbit6.genB","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.Orbit6.genB","kind":"def","line":113,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/Orbit6Braid.lean#L113-L131","doc":"`ρ(σ_{i+1})` on the same orbit: pairs `{0,1}`, `{2,3}`, `{4,5}` with parameters\n`a₂`, `a₃`, `a₁`.  A **different** pairing from `genA` — this is what makes the orbit\nirreducible to `2 × 2` blocks. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.Orbit6.genA","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.Orbit6.genA","kind":"def","line":89,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/Orbit6Braid.lean#L89-L111","doc":"`ρ(σ_i)` on a size-`6` orbit: pairs `{0,2}`, `{1,4}`, `{3,5}` with parameters\n`a₁`, `a₃`, `a₂`.  The partner entry of a block is `c - a`, per `seminormalParam_add_neg`;\nthe lower-left entry is `1 + a·(c-a)`, per `seminormalBlock`.\n\nDefined by a match on the index values rather than as a `!![…]` literal: entry lookup is\nthen a single iota reduction, where the literal form forces `simp` through a chain of\n`vecCons` unfoldings and blows up (the `6 × 6` triple product has 1296 scalar terms). ","signatureChunk":0}]}